@Mike R - You're describing hypogeal germination, where the sole (or primary) function of the cotyledons is food storage. Acorns are a great example.

But many plants are epigeal, meaning the cotyledons are photosynthetic and produce "food" that way. If the seed germinated underground, these are the leaves that push up above the soil soon after germination. Beans, sunflowers, and so on are common examples. 

Both types of germination are common -- I have no sense which is more common.

@Gretchen
